@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "SQL Server Fundamentals";
}

<section>

    <!-- =========================================================
         PAGE INTRODUCTION
         ========================================================= -->

    <div class="mb-5">

        <h1 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            SQL Server Fundamentals
        </h1>

        <p class="lead text-muted">
            SQL Server fundamentals introduce the basic concepts required
            to work with databases, tables, SQL queries and the SQL Server
            database engine.
        </p>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================================================
         WHAT IS SQL SERVER?
         ========================================================= -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            What is SQL Server?
        </h2>

        <p>
            SQL Server is a relational database management system (RDBMS)
            developed by Microsoft. It is used to store, manage and
            retrieve structured data.
        </p>

        <p>
            SQL Server uses SQL (Structured Query Language) to communicate
            with databases and perform operations such as inserting,
            retrieving, updating and deleting data.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================================================
         DATABASE
         ========================================================= -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            What is a Database?
        </h2>

        <p>
            A database is an organized collection of data that allows
            information to be stored and managed efficiently.
        </p>

        <p>
            In SQL Server, a database can contain tables, views,
            stored procedures, functions and other database objects.
        </p>

        <div class="alert alert-primary border-0">

            <h5 class="fw-bold">
                Example
            </h5>

            <p class="mb-0">
                A college database may contain student information,
                course information, faculty information and examination
                records.
            </p>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================================================
         TABLE
         ========================================================= -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            What is a Table?
        </h2>

        <p>
            A table is used to store data in rows and columns.
            Each table represents a particular type of information.
        </p>

        <p>
            For example, a <strong>Students</strong> table can contain
            information about students.
        </p>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>StudentId</th>
                        <th>Name</th>
                        <th>Email</th>
                        <th>Course</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td>1</td>
                        <td>Rahul</td>
                        <td>rahul@example.com</td>
                        <td>.NET</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>2</td>
                        <td>Priya</td>
                        <td>priya@example.com</td>
                        <td>Java</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>3</td>
                        <td>Amit</td>
                        <td>amit@example.com</td>
                        <td>Python</td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================================================
         ROWS AND COLUMNS
         ========================================================= -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Rows and Columns
        </h2>

        <p>
            A table is made up of rows and columns.
        </p>

        <ul>

            <li class="mb-2">
                <strong>Column:</strong> Defines a particular type of data.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                <strong>Row:</strong> Represents one complete record.
            </li>

        </ul>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================================================
         SQL
         ========================================================= -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            What is SQL?
        </h2>

        <p>
            SQL stands for Structured Query Language. It is used to
            communicate with relational databases.
        </p>

        <p>
            SQL allows developers and database administrators to perform
            different operations on database data.
        </p>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>Operation</th>
                        <th>Purpose</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>SELECT</strong></td>
                        <td>Retrieve data from a database.</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>INSERT</strong></td>
                        <td>Add new data to a table.</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>UPDATE</strong></td>
                        <td>Modify existing data.</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>DELETE</strong></td>
                        <td>Remove data from a table.</td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================================================
         BASIC SQL QUERY
         ========================================================= -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Basic SQL Query
        </h2>

        <p>
            The following query retrieves all records from the Students
            table.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT * FROM Students;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================================================
         SQL SERVER BASIC FLOW
         ========================================================= -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            How SQL Server Works
        </h2>

        <p>
            A client application sends a SQL query to SQL Server.
            SQL Server processes the query and communicates with the
            database engine to retrieve or modify the required data.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL Server
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0">
                                                <code>Client Application
                                                         ↓
                                                      SQL Query
                                                          ↓
                                                      SQL Server
                                                          ↓
                                                     Database Engine
                                                          ↓
                                                       Database
                                                          ↓
                                                        Result</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p class="mt-3">
            The Database Engine processes the SQL statement and returns
            the requested result to the client application.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================================================
         DATABASE OBJECTS
         ========================================================= -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Common SQL Server Database Objects
        </h2>

        <p>
            SQL Server databases contain different objects that are used
            to store data and implement database functionality.
        </p>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>Object</th>
                        <th>Purpose</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>Tables</strong></td>
                        <td>Store data in rows and columns.</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>Views</strong></td>
                        <td>Provide a virtual representation of data.</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>Stored Procedures</strong></td>
                        <td>Store reusable SQL statements and logic.</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>Functions</strong></td>
                        <td>Perform reusable operations and return values.</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>Triggers</strong></td>
                        <td>Automatically execute in response to database events.</td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================================================
         CRUD OPERATIONS
         ========================================================= -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            CRUD Operations
        </h2>

        <p>
            CRUD represents the four basic operations performed on
            database data.
        </p>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>Operation</th>
                        <th>SQL Command</th>
                        <th>Purpose</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td>Create</td>
                        <td>INSERT</td>
                        <td>Add new records.</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>Read</td>
                        <td>SELECT</td>
                        <td>Retrieve records.</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>Update</td>
                        <td>UPDATE</td>
                        <td>Modify records.</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>Delete</td>
                        <td>DELETE</td>
                        <td>Remove records.</td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================================================
         SUMMARY
         ========================================================= -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <div class="alert alert-primary border-0">

            <h2 class="fw-bold">
                Summary
            </h2>

            <p class="mb-0">
                In this section, we learned the fundamentals of SQL Server,
                databases, tables, rows and columns, SQL queries, database
                objects and CRUD operations. These concepts provide the
                foundation for working with SQL Server databases.
            </p>

        </div>

    </section>


